  {% set redteagoPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Saily</strong> if you'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling and want that bundled in with your data. Pick <strong>RedteaGO</strong> if you just want the lowest realistic starting price on a data-only eSIM and don't care about security extras. This isn't close on price: RedteaGO's entry tiers start under a dollar, while Saily's cheapest per-GB rate is several times that once you account for what each one actually includes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Security Suite vs a Bare-Bones Data Plan</h2>
  <p>
    Saily is NordVPN's eSIM spin-off, built by Nord Security rather than merely partnered with them, and every plan ships with ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool baked in. It also sells one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, instead of a separate profile per region. RedteaGO skips all of that: its plans are fixed data buckets (2GB, 5GB, and similar) with no unlimited tier we could find, and by RedteaGO's own FAQ, calls and texts are only available on plans specifically labeled "Calls/Texts Available," not as standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    What RedteaGO does have going for it is the starting price. Its own shop advertises eSIMs from as little as $0.52 for a Europe-wide plan and $0.70 for a US, Japan, or Thailand plan, both smallest-data-tier prices across a claimed 180+ countries and regions. Saily's plans start around $3.99 for a 1GB US eSIM, before its 15% code. You're not comparing the same product at two prices: you're comparing a security-and-convenience bundle against a stripped-down data allotment.
  </p>

