    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is ours. Saily and RedteaGO don't run the kind of affiliate program that supports our own code the way Holafly or Ubigi do, so both are the best public codes we've verified. Saily's 15% applies storewide; RedteaGO's 5% is more modest but stacks on an already-low base price.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Saily or RedteaGO the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>Multi-country trip where you don't want a new eSIM at every border</li>
        <li>You want a real refund option if the eSIM never activates</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with RedteaGO if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lowest realistic starting price and don't care about security extras</li>
        <li>You know roughly how much data you need and are fine buying it in a fixed block</li>
        <li>You need to hotspot a laptop off your phone, confirmed to work everywhere RedteaGO operates</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>RedteaGO's data top-ups are non-refundable once activated.</strong> Its own FAQ states the plan activates immediately on purchase, with no refund on unused data. Saily, by contrast, offers a full refund if the eSIM was never installed or activated within 30 days, or if less than 1% of the data was used.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily is blocked for in-country purchase in Turkey.</strong> Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK) blocked Saily's website and app for buying or topping up while physically in the country starting mid-2025 (an eSIM bought and installed before arrival still works). RedteaGO faces no such restriction and is one of the cheaper options specifically for Turkey, at $1.50/GB.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. If neither pricing model fits, <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>'s flat unlimited rate is worth a look for heavy data users who don't want to track either a GB meter or a fair-use throttle.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Saily vs RedteaGO FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Saily or RedteaGO</h3>
      <p>RedteaGO, on a straight per-GB basis for most destinations. Saily costs more per gigabyte but bundles a VPN-style security suite and ad blocking that RedteaGO doesn't offer at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one offer unlimited data</h3>
      <p>Saily does, with a daily fair-use allowance (5GB full speed in most destinations, 3GB in some, then throttled to 1Mbps). RedteaGO doesn't: everything on its site is sold as a fixed data bucket.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one works better for calls and texts</h3>
      <p>Neither is built around voice by default. Saily is data-only across the board. RedteaGO includes calls/texts only on plans specifically labeled "Calls/Texts Available," plus a separate Call & Text eSIM package.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund on either if I change my mind</h3>
      <p>Saily, yes, within 30 days if the eSIM was never installed or activated, or if under 1% of the data was used. RedteaGO, no: its FAQ states data top-ups are non-refundable once the plan activates.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

